## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limit of a sequence)
## Date(09/16/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'identify bounded', 'identify monotonicity')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", 'parserPopUp.pl', "PGcourse.pl");

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => "Real");

$a = random(2, 6);

$popup1 = DropDown([ 'is', 'is not' ], 0);
$popup2 =
    DropDown([ "monotone increasing", "monotone decreasing", "not monotone" ],
    "not monotone");

BEGIN_PGML

For the sequence [`\{a_n\} = \{[$a]\sin(n)\}`], determine whether the sequence is bounded and whether it is eventually monotone increasing, monotone decreasing, or not monotone. 

 a) [`\{a_n\} = \{[$a]\sin(n)\}`] [___]{$popup1} bounded.

 b) [`\{a_n\} = \{[$a]\sin(n)\}`] is eventually [___]{$popup2} .
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Because [`\sin(n)`] is bounded, [`[$a]\sin(n)`] is also bounded.

b) [`[$a] \sin(n)`] oscillates as [`n`] increases.  Therefore, it is not monotone.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
